IDEA 1: Late LeGrad selection — For the student’s predicted class \(c=\arg\max z\), compute \(G^\ell=\partial z_c/\partial A^\ell\) for blocks 10–12 using `autograd.grad(..., retain_graph=True)`; score patch \(i\) by \(s_i=\sum_{\ell=10}^{12}\operatorname{mean}_{h,q}[G^\ell_{hqi}]_+\).
Keep exactly the top-K patches: this reads positive prediction sensitivity across heads and queries, without multiplying by attention magnitude or introducing spatial preferences. — basis: Bousselham et al., [LeGrad, ICCV 2025](https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/ICCV2025/html/Bousselham_LeGrad_An_Explainability_Method_for_Vision_Transformers_via_Feature_Formation_ICCV_2025_paper.html); its localization and perturbation results support sensitivity as an explanatory signal. Restricting it to late layers and using the final prediction are proposed adaptations; superiority on CUB remains untested. — closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [DCP, EMNLP 2025](https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1074/) uses gradient-weighted attention and textual guidance; this uses unweighted attention gradients of student species predictions to select teacher inputs. — extra FLOPs: approximately 0.5–1.3 GFLOPs/image for one backward traversal through the final three blocks, plus negligible reductions/top-K; architecture-dependent and fully chargeable.

IDEA 2: Factorized concept coverage — From student block-8 patch vectors \(X\), factor the nonnegative matrix \([X_+,(-X)_+]\approx HW\) using rank-6 NMF with 20 multiplicative-update iterations; normalize each column of \(H\) to maximum 1, producing six candidate concept maps.
Let \(a_i\) be student last-block mean CLS attention and \(w_c\propto\sum_i a_iH_{ic}/(\epsilon+\sum_iH_{ic})\); greedily select exactly K patches by marginal gain in \(F(S)=\sum_c w_c\log(1+\sum_{i\in S}H_{ic})\), rewarding additional evidence for underrepresented concepts without coordinates. — inspired by: Collins, Achanta and Süsstrunk, [Deep Feature Factorization for Concept Discovery, ECCV 2018, arXiv:1806.10206](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.10206). — why it transfers: DFF demonstrated unsupervised object-part discovery from internal activations; among the retrieved mechanisms, it offers a cheap way to preserve complementary bird parts through explicit concept maps, although the ViT adaptation and CUB gains remain hypotheses. — queries tried: neural network explanations complementary evidence redundant features submodular coverage; experimental design diverse measurements preserve Fisher information log determinant sensor selection; discover semantic object parts hidden activations nonnegative factorization; contrastive explanations fine grained classification why this rather than that evidence; information bottleneck minimal sufficient explanation neural network internal representations; sparse reconstruction representative examples archetypal analysis neural network explanations — closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [CoverPruner, EMNLP 2026](https://aclanthology.org/2026.emnlp-main.349/) covers discarded token representations; this balances coverage of interpretable activation factors, weighted entirely by student computation. — extra FLOPs: roughly 0.15 GFLOPs/image at width \(d=384\), including NMF and greedy selection; no additional model forward or backward pass.